Audit every document against the code, and correct what did not survive it

Eleven agents each took one document and verified its claims against the
source rather than against the neighbouring prose. What follows is what
did not survive.

The engine does not enforce a 0.75 load factor. Five documents said it
did, and the sole 0.75 computation in the whole engine is inside BENCH
(parse.inc:2005); INSERT refuses only when a probe of the entire slot
table finds nowhere to put a row. Following that thread further, the
per-tier row ceiling we publish to customers is enforced nowhere at all:
MaxRows appears in provisioner.go's tier table and in a test asserting
it equals three quarters of the slots, and nowhere else. It is never
sent to the container and never checked on insert. The documents now say
plainly that this is a published ceiling rather than an enforced one.
Whether to enforce it, publish the real capacity, or keep it as advice
is a product decision and is left open.

The binary size was wrong everywhere except ENGINE.md. Rebuilding from
source gives 43,741 and 52,205; four files carried 43,749 and 52,221,
agreeing with each other because they had been copied from each other.
Agreement between documents is not evidence.

SECURITY.md described a token-rotation gap that has been closed: the
pending token is sealed with AES-GCM, bound to the database id, and
expires in fifteen minutes. A threat model that overstates a fixed
vulnerability gets its known-gaps section disbelieved wholesale, so this
matters as much as an understatement would.

CDC.md gains the mechanism it lacked, and two diagrams. It also gains
three corrections that only reading the assembly could produce:
commit_seq is dense rather than merely increasing, and the scan refuses
anything that is not exactly last+1; CDCTRIM exists, which the document
denied; and a RESET frame is published after the table is replaced
rather than before, its safety resting on reset_pending in the header.

ENGINE.md now establishes ACID letter by letter with citations, and says
where each guarantee stops. Isolation is the interesting one: writers
are serialised by a lock, so they are serialisable, but a reader mapping
the file shared can observe a half-applied state mid-scan and is saved
by a before/after sequence fence that discards and replays rather than
by any property of the write path.

COMMANDS.md was checked by running the engine. Fifteen documented
outputs did not match what it prints.

workload/README.md still opened with "Nothing here is built yet".

